<Base title="Data Engineering & AI Search Projects | Federica Iengo" description="Data engineering and AI Search projects, including GEO, AEO, SEO, SQL, Python, Snowflake, dbt and reproducible technical evidence.">
<section class="intro">
<p class="eyebrow">Projects</p>
<h1>Work that can be inspected,<br /><span class="title-accent">not just described.</span></h1>
<p class="lead">From building trustworthy data pipelines to studying how AI Search finds and cites information, each project starts with a practical question and shows what can actually be checked.</p>
</section>

<section class="evidence-note" aria-label="Project evidence and status"><p class="eyebrow">Reading guide</p><h2>What is demonstrated, and what is still being tested.</h2><p>These are independent portfolio projects, not client engagements. A research protocol, working analysis pipeline or proposed optimization is not evidence of improved search visibility or commercial performance. Each case study identifies its current status and distinguishes source material, analysis and measured outcomes.</p></section>

<section class="reader-path" aria-label="How to read these projects">
<p class="eyebrow">For recruiters and curious readers</p>
<h2>What does this work actually demonstrate?</h2>
<p>You do not need to know SQL, Python or dbt to evaluate the outcomes. Each project starts with the practical question, then explains what was built, what can be checked and what remains uncertain.</p>
<div class="grid">
<article class="card"><h3>1 · The problem</h3><p>What decision, information gap or reliability issue motivated the work?</p></article>
<article class="card"><h3>2 · The evidence</h3><p>Which data, source code, tests or documented observations support the findings?</p></article>
<article class="card"><h3>3 · The limits</h3><p>What was verified, what is still a proposal and what cannot yet be concluded?</p></article>
</div>
<p class="reader-link"><a href="/research/">Explore the research method →</a></p>
</section>

